## 1. Introduction
Hollow Multishelled Structures (HoMSs) are a major type of burgeoning advanced nanostructure which have at least two shells with corresponding internal cavities, and the multiple shells are arranged in order from outside to inside.1 Different from the random aggregation of nanoparticles or the singleshelled hollow structure, HoMSs have the irreplaceable advantages arising from the temporal-spatial ordering structure.2 More specifically, materials with well-separated shells can perform different functionalities within one little micro-nano system. More importantly, the physical/chemical processes take place on or through each shell sequentially with specific orders from outside to inside or in reverse.2 This is crucial for applications such as selective cascade reactions, smart drug release, stepwise separation and sequential light harvesting.3 Since 2009, our group developed a universal and facile method for the synthesis of HoMSs, namely sequential templating approach (STA). By precisely controlling the reaction thermodynamics and kinetics, the micro-nano structures of HoMS have been controlled.4-8 Afterwards, the breakthrough in synthetic methodology make HoMS a hotspot in material science rapidly, and various related applications have been boomingly developed.7, 9-14 HoMS material has been widely recognized as one of the most promising functional materials.15 However, the precise fabrication of HoMSs (with the composition of metal oxide/sulfide/phosphide, while not the MOF-based HoMS, unless otherwise specified) in molecular level is still confronted with challenges. The micro-level HoMS particle is assembled by nanoparticles, which leads to irregular channel structure and broad pore size distribution, thus bringing a difficulty in the study of mass transport in HoMSs. Also, the exposed facet of these nanoparticles is still difficult to control until our recent report using topological chemical reaction to convert metal organic frameworks (MOFs) to HoMSs.16 MOF is a kind of porous crystalline material formed by the linkage of metal ions or clusters with organic ligands.17-20 The topologically ordered crystalline structure endows MOFs with the advantages of uniform and adjustable pore size, ordered atom arrangement, and tailorable functionality, making them promising in a wide range of applications.21-25
In this case, it is of conceptual novelty to integrate MOFs with HoMSs, which may bring more possibilities to guide the material design and exploit new applications. Firstly, the specific property of coordinate metal atom arrangement and ordered pores in MOFs can endow the derived HoMS material with a controllable micro/nano structure; Secondly, in reverse, fabricating MOFs with HoMS can endow MOFs with the temporal-spatial ordering property, optimized mass transfer and abundant and accessible reaction sites, extending the applications; Thirdly, the rational design of heterogeneous
HoMS with MOF can provide the syngeneic effects for a certain reaction. Note that the speciality of MOF-based HoMSs is the cavity between the shells, which is quite different from compact MOF-based core-shell structure. These cavities can perform as a micro reactor to enrich substrate, raise partial pressure, promote diffusion, and further accelerate reaction. Meanwhile, this unique hollow multishelled structure can provide larger effective surface area for reactions. Thus, the merits from both the materials (MOF) side and the structure (HoMS) side make a perfect cooperation to accommodate properties that may not be achieved elsewhere. Excellent works from all around the world have attracted enormous attention from diverse disciplines. In this case, it is about time to survey the progress of related researches, exploit the fabrication approach for combination of MOFs and HoMSs, discuss the structureinduced property, and furthermore provide new perspectives and directions to further boom the development of this field. Herein, this perspective article systematically covers the correlations between MOF and HoMS, which is divided into three parts, including coating HoMSs with MOFs, deriving HoMSs from MOFs, and fabricating hollow multi-shelled MOFs. At the end, we also put forward our personal outlook of the future opportunities and challenges in this promising research field.
## 2. HoMSs with MOF coating
The most straightforward and rational approach to hybrid the advantages of HoMSs and MOFs is to coat MOFs on HoMSs, named as MOF-coated HoMS. Because MOFs have good affinity to various substrates,26-28 it is not a big challenge to fabricate the MOF-coated HoMS hybrids experimentally. The first paper in this topic is published in 2019 by Wang’s group.29 Take the synthesis of HoMSs $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{M L}\cdot100(\mathsf{F}$ e) as an example, tripleshelled $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ hollow structures were synthesized first with STA, and then the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ HoMSs were modified with PVP, which was conducive to capturing MOF precursors for heterogeneous nucleation. After soaking the HoMSs into $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ solution and ${\sf H}_{3}{\sf B}{\sf T}{\sf C}$ solution alternatively for several times, a dense MOF coating can be uniformly formed on the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ HoMSs (Figure 1), and the thickness of MOF shells could be easily controlled by adjusting the number of repeated cycles. This method was also universal, which can be extended to the casing of other HoMSs (e.g. ${\mathsf{T i O}}_{2},$ NiO, $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ ).
Furthermore, a precise post-treatment can transform the coated MOF into porous carbons30, metal oxides31, metal active centres32 or single atoms33, but they can retain the structural information of MOFs and demonstrate positive effect on the
performance of substrate materials for various applications. For instance, the aforementioned MOF-coated HoMS, i.e., $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{M l L}\cdot100(\mathsf{F e})$ , was post-treated by a two-step calcination, i.e., first in ${\sf N}_{2}$ protection and then in air. By precisely tailoring the pyrolysis process of MOFs, HoMSs in heterogeneous casing, such as $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ (MOF)-HoMS and $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e O}_{\mathsf{x}^{-}}\mathsf{C}$ (MOF)- HoMS, were obtained. The characteristics of large surface areas and porous structures of MOFs can be largely retained in their derived casings. The specific surface area of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}(\mathsf{M O F})$ - HoMS was eight times larger than that of pure $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ HoMS, and $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}(\mathsf{M O F})\ –\mathsf{H o M S}$ also had increased pore volume and porosity. As a consequence, the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}(\mathsf{M O F})$ -HoMS hybrid worked as a superior anode material in lithium ion batteries, which showed higher specific surface area and more lithium-storage sites, thus larger storage capacity can be provided. It also demonstrated excellent structural stability, providing promoted reversibility of active materials. The cycling ability improved dramatically. The retention capacity after 100 cycles of the $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ (MOF)-HoMS increased eightfold compared with $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ (particle)-HoMS, which demonstrated superiority of the casing originated from MOFs. The porous hierarchical structure in $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ (MOF)-HoMS also facilitated the ion diffusion and decreased the charge resistance, leading to the significantly improved rate performance. Specifically, the capacity of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}@\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}(\mathsf{M O F})-$ HoMS was tripled that of $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ HoMSs at the same current density of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ because of the rapid charge/ion transfer within the porous and “breathable” MOF-derived casing.29
Moreover, the hybrid can also provide more space to store the reactants, which benefits reactant accumulation in the local environment.34 For instance, Prof. Yaghi discovered that although MOF is not active for gas-phase hydrogenative conversion of methyl-cyclopentane, it increases the activity by enhancing local concentrations of reactant and ${\sf H}_{2}$ inside the
MOF crystals and endows the catalyst with unusual selectivity via kinetic change of adsorption geometries of transition states or intermediate species within the pores of MOF.35 It can be predicted that hybridizing the advantages of HoMSs, such as the efficient light utilization36, abundant effective surface areas37 and fast mass transmission38, with MOFs can further broaden their applications.
## 3.  MOF-derived HoMSs
STA refers to the approach which starts from the template rich in precursor, and then this template can direct the process sequentially for multiple times during its removal. The widely used templates include the metal-rich carbonaceous spheres39 or polymer particles40, and metal-polymer hybrid particles41. Among them, MOFs, with beautifully designed structure and metal-rich composition, can be considered as an excellent template for STA. Compared with conventional templates, MOFs show several characteristics in the fabrication of HoMSs: (1) MOFs can work as both metal-rich precursors and templates, thus there is no necessity for further ion absorption process; (2) the metal motif dispersion can be considered as relatively uniform within the MOF, without the concentration gradient; (3) the obtained materials normally resemble the shape of parent MOFs to get a variety of morphologies; (4) the MOFderived materials often have well-defined hierarchical pore structure and a easily tailored composition. Above all, MOFs have been highlighted in recent years as unique precursors for HoMSs with design possibilities. Moreover, we would like to mention the precise control on the reaction at the interface is significantly important for the formation of hollow materials with a certain micro/nano structure. Only the accurate control on the HoMS synthesis process can release all the structural potentials of MOFs.
Calcination/annealing is the most common way to synthesize metal oxide HoMS through STA.1 During calcination, a temperature gradient along the radial direction was established. The outer parts of MOFs firstly discomposed to form a rigid metal oxide shell while the inner part remained unpyrolyzed. Afterwards, the continuous mass loss and volume shrinkage led to the separation of metal oxide shells from inner MOF cores, and the inner MOF cores can act as the followingVtiewmArptilcalet eOsn itnoe repeat this process for several times,DOrIe:s1u0l.1ti0n3g9/Di0nSCh0o1l2l8o4wJ structures with multiple shells.42, 43 Moreover, because of the flexibility in chemical composition and structure in MOF, the composition of HoMS can be easily tuned.44, 45 As reported, metal oxide HoMSs with different compositions, doping elements and mixtures can be obtained. Especially, MOF with a variety of morphologies can be readily prepared46-48, thus HoMSs with distinctive morphologies other than sphere are expected to be fabricated through STA by using MOF as templates. For example, Co/Mn-ZIFs dodecahedra were firstly obtained at room temperature by co-precipitating of metal salts and linker in methanol. After a continuous two-step calcination, manganese-cobalt oxide HoMSs with the dodecahedron shape were prepared, and the shell number can be controlled from single to triple by adjusting the calcination conditions.42
The most significant feather of adopting MOFs as template is using their topological structure of atomic arrangement. The arranging pattern of atoms in MOFs may have an unignorable impact on the atom arrangement of the final product. By controlling the calcination parameters (such as oxygen partial pressure, and heating rate), the surface crystalline structure of obtained materials can be regulated. After deeply studying the Co atom arrangement in ZIF67, we noticed that the spatial distribution of Co atoms within exposed (001) and (011) facets of ZIF67 demonstrated an arrangement similar to (111) facet of $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ . Hence, during the thermal decomposition, Co atoms tended to topologically shift within crystal lattice to form $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ with (111) facets exposing dominantly, instead of relocating through complicated rotation, torsion, and migration to expose other facets like (311), which is the dominant facet prepared by other methods. Because of this so-called topological transmission, the atoms transferred in desired directions to selectively expose certain crystal facets (111). Consequently, triple-shelled $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ hollow dodecahedron with a unique shell preferably exposing (111) facets was prepared. Considering the $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ (111) facets have stronger interactions with $\mathsf{C O}_{2},$ it can possess higher activity towards photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. In this case, owing to the facet control, the activity of MOF-derived HoMSs is about 5- and 3-times higher than that of $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles (NPs) and $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ hollow spheres prepared by carbon sphere templates, respectively.16
Another promising factor of different MOFs is the different graphitization degree during calcination, which can be considered as a unique method for HoMS fabrication. By using this knowledge, Zhang et al. firstly prepared five layered ZIF67@ZIF8@ZIF67@ZIF8@ZIF67 solid structures through shell-by-shell epitaxial growth. Then the obtained materials were heated in nitrogen atmosphere. During the pyrolysis, the organic component in ZIF8 converted into amorphous carbon, while the organic component in ZIF67 converted into porous carbon with relatively higher degree of graphitization. Later on, the inner amorphous carbon underwent an inside-out Ostwald ripening process, diffused outward and further graphitized into dense shells, which was catalysed by Co NPs derived from the exterior ZIF67 component, leaving interior voids between the shells. By controlling the shell-by-shell coating procedures, different multi-layered structures can be easily synthesized, resulting in different complex nitrogen-doped hollow porous carbon architectures, including multi-shell, yolk-shell, and yolkmulti-shell structures. Owing to their hierarchical
