Furthermore, HoMSs with more complex architectures can be achieved through controllable processing reaction steps. For example, Zhang et al. utilized two separated reaction to prepare unique double-shelled HoMSs with inner shell of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O H}})_{2}$ and outer shell of NiCo-LDH. By depositing ZIF67 into the ethanol solution of ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2},$ LDH shells were formed on the outside of ZIF67 rhombic dodecahedra. Then, the single-shelled ZIF67@LDH was placed into ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{M o O}}_{4}$ aqueous solution to trigger the second reaction and fabricate double-shelled $C o(O H)_{2}@L D H$ hollow polyhedra.52 Hierarchical double-shelled hollow structures with different shell subunits can be obtained as well through delicate manipulation of the template-engaged reaction. Starting from ZIF67 nanocubes, Hu et al. managed the interfacial reaction with water to prepare $Z I F67/C o(O H)_{2}$ yolkshelled structures and further transformed them into CoS double-shelled structure using a sequential reaction with ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S}}$ . Interestingly, the inner CoS shell was composed of NPs, whereas the outer shell was consisted of assembled nanosheets, despite of the same composition.53
It can be observed from the aforementioned examples, the precise reaction control at the interface (liquid/solid, gas/solid) is significantly important to direct the precise HoMS fabrication. In this case, by combining the interfacial reaction method with the calcination approach, complex HoMSs with heterogeneous composition in different shells can be constructed. For example, Lu et al. prepared triple-shelled $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ HoMS, in which the outermost shell was ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{V}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{8}$ while the inner two shells were $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ .54 In detail, firstly, these ZIF67 nanocubes were dispersed in absolute ethanol solution of vanadium oxytriisopropoxide (VOT) and continuously stirred for 20 min to form a clear solution. Then a ZIF67@amorphous- ${\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{V}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{8}$ yolkshell-structure was obtained after a solvothermal treatment of the aforementioned solution with the VOT concentration of 50 $\upmu\upiota$ as shown in Step 1 (Figure 4), during which, vanadate anion $(\mathsf{V O}_{4}{}^{3-})$ generated from VOT is considered to gradually replace the 2-methylimidazole anion in ZIF67 by ion exchange to form amorphous- ${\cdot\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}}$ shell. Meanwhile, the continuous consumption of ZIF67 core results in a gap space between the newly formed amorphous- $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ shell and the remaining core. Afterward, a thermal calcination in air was applied to convert amorphous- $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ shell into crystalline one and ZIF67 core was converted into $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ hollow shells. As such, tripleshelled $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ nanoboxes can be eventually obtained. By precisely tuning the VOT concentration, two other hollow nanostructures, i.e., double-shelled $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}@\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ and singleshelled $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{V}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{8}$ nanoboxes, can also be synthesized. The different compositions of different shells can be easily observed from HR-TEM and EDS element mapping.
## 4. HoMS-MOFs
In reverse, MOFs with hollow multi-shelled structure (HoMSMOFs) can endow the unique properties of HoMS to MOFs. As previously mentioned, MOF is one of the most attractive materials for great promising in a variety of important applications, including adsorption55, storage56, separation57,detection58, photoluminescence59, catalysis60 and biomedicine61. Great efforts have been devoted to the design and preparation of MOFs with exquisite topologic structures to improve performance or exploit new properties. Especially, the unique distinguished micor-nanostructural features of HoMSs are conductive to energy storage62, absorption63, sensing64, catalysis65, drug delivery66, etc. The fabrication of HoMS-MOFs may reinforce the usefulness of MOF materials and expand the scope of utilization of these materials.
Hard-templating methods are the most conceptually straightforward route, and HoMS-MOFs can be achieved through shell-by-shell assembly and selective etching. The synthesis always requires multi-step coating alternately with target materials and interlayer materials, followed by selectively removing templates and interlayers according to different stability.67 A typical example was demonstrated by Tsung’s group. Because ZIF67 and ZIF8 shared the same topology and crystal structure but different metal nodes, they can epitaxially grow on each other easily. The onion-like multilayered ZIF67@ZIF8 solid nanocubes can be prepared by the shell-by-shell overgrowth. As ZIF67 was less stable than ZIF8 in water/methanol solution, Co ions were released due to the dissolution of ZIF67, while ZIF8 parts remained stable. When
ZIF67 was completely removed, leaving cavities bVietwwAretieclne OZnIliFn8e layers, i.e., the HoMS ZIF8 was obtained. IDmOpI:o1r0t.a10n3tl9y/,Dt0hSeC0m12u8lt4iJshelled MOFs can work as a novel host matrix to confine different kinds of guests at individual locations to mimic biological structures. More specifically, the guests originally immobilized in ZIF8 layers are still there, while the guests originally immobilized in ZIF67 layers are released and confined in the cavities between ZIF8 shells. The chromophore molecules and metal nanoparticles (NPs) can be precisely controlled either anchored on the MOF shells or in the cavities between shells. In this case, Förster resonance energy transfer (FRET) molecules, rhodamine 6G (R6G) and 7-amino-4-(trifluoromethyl)coumarin (C-151) were selected as the molecule guests, and Pd NPs were selected as the heterogeneous guests. When the guests were fixed in MOFs, the energy transfer was promoted; while if the guests were encapsulated in cavities, the native flexibility of surface functional groups can be reserved. The former was essential to applications related to energy transfer (ET) process, like photo-fluorescence. As NPs and molecules usually provide different functions, the integration of them into one system with the controlled ET is reasonable. If R6G molecules and Pd NPs were located in one cavity, the fluorescence was largely reduced due to the quenching effect. However, when Pd NPs and R6G were separated into different cavities within HoMS, enhanced fluorescence was detected because the quenching process was effectively inhibited by the separation of ZIF8 shell. In this way, the mobility of guests can be switched according to different application demands. Thus, the Guest-to-Host and Guest-to-Guest interactions can be regulated. This work enabled the precise separation control of multi-guests within HoMSs, providing a new multifunctional platform.67
Similar to the shell-by-shell formation, fabricating the alternate MOF@MOP (MOP $\mathbf{\bar{\rho}}=\mathbf{\rho}$ metal–organic polyhedra) structure can be also used to construct HoMS-MOFs.68 For instance, Choe’s group reported the synthetic strategy to prepare multi-shelled hollow MOFs via sequential interfacial reaction using MOP as precursor. Cuboctahedron MOP (cuoMOP) was selected as starting material, it transformed into ubtMOF by connecting adjacent precursors with dabco (1,4- diazabicyclooctanetriethylenediamine) linkers. The linker insertion reaction took place on the surface of MOP, and the asformed MOF layers prevented the linkers from penetration and prohibited further reaction with the inner part. Therefore, a core-shell structure of MOP@MOF is formed. Then another layer of MOP can be coated on the surface of MOF through the epitaxial growth. After another surficial insertion reaction, the outermost layer of MOP converted into MOF. This process can be repeated for several times and multi-layered matryoshkalike core-shell structure (denoted as MOF@MOP $@$ MOF@MOP) was formed. Because MOP was more vulnerable to chemical etching than MOF. The MOP layers can be removed under a proper etching condition, while MOF layers remained. Thus, a HoMS-MOF was achieved.69
Different from the selective etching of different MOFs, the fabrication of the hollow MOFs starting with single composition precursor is quite constructive,70-72 after knowing the inhomogeneity in MOF due to the unevenly distributed defects. Normally, the core of the as-synthesized materials has more defects, because of the rapid nucleation and growth at the initial stage.73 And the defect-rich part was less stable than wellcrystallized part. The MOF crystal was in fact a core-shell structure that is expressed as less stable (LS)@more stable (MS). Under a proper chemical etching condition, the inner core can be removed while the outer shell remained, giving rise to a hollow structure. Impressively, if the shell-by-shell crystal growth repeated for several times, multi-layered core-shell labelled as $\mathsf{L S@M S@L S@M S@L S@M S}$ materials can be achieved. After etching away the LS part, multi-shelled MOFs can be obtained. Huo et al. 74 chose MIL-101(Cr) as an example. After putting pre-synthesized LS@MS MIL-101 crystals into the mother solution for another hydrothermal reaction, the additional layer of MIL-101 can be epitaxially grown on the former truncated octahedral particles. The newly formed MOF layer also had a LS@MS structure. Subsequently, acetic acid was added to preferentially etch away LS part, creating interior hollow cavity. Additionally, the thickness of each shell can be tuned by controlling the crystal growth time and the cavity space varied with the different etching time. This rational strategy can effectively prepare HoMS-MOFs with singlecrystalline shells. HoMS-MIL101 has shown better conversion rate and higher selectivity towards benzaldehyde for styrene oxidation reaction compared with solid or less-shelled counterparts. The phase, morphology and catalytic performance remained unchanged after five reusing cycles. The structural advantages of HoMSs provide more exposed active sites, which can improve the adsorption of reactants. Following a similar shell-by-shell growth and subsequent etching method, other kinds of HoMS-MOFs can be fabricated as well.75,76 From the study, researchers discovered that HoMS-MOFs not only had better adsorption capacity but also had a much slower release in the desorption isotherms, suggesting promises in applications favouring slow guest release, like drug delivery.
## 5. Outlook and Conclusion
Both the MOF materials and the HoMSs possess diverse intrinsic physical and chemical characteristics that are interesting from both fundamental and practical perspectives. The combination of these two concepts can give both additional properties, which has shown its advantages in catalysis, energy conversion & storage and adsorption. However, it should be noted that this emerging topic still needs a special attention in both synthetic methodology and applications, and a plenty of space still remains to be explored, including: (1) Preparing HoMSs with more complex structures and compositions with various MOF precursors. For example, building up the heterogeneous inorganic HoMSs is quite attractive. Great efforts should be contributed to putting this proof-of-concept into practice by using the heterogeneous MOFs. For instance, to build heterogeneous MOFs with different metal elements inside out, and then to transfer them under proper conditions will give rise to heterogeneous HoMSs with different compositions in each shell. The hetero-HoMSs can successively absorb sunlight with different wavelengths in different shells from outside to inside (sequential solar-light-harvesting), broaden the absorption range of solar spectrum, and enhance the utilization of light.77 (2) For HoMS-MOFs, besides the shell-by-shell approach, new methods are still in urgent need. For example, we can predict the solid HoMS can also be considered as a precursor and template to direct the HoMS-MOF formation. Decades of study in HoMSs has provided a huge number of HoMSs with various compositions, which can all be adopted for the MOF synthesis. Depending on the composition of HoMS (including the homo- and heterogeneous composition insideout), the composition in HoMS-MOF can be well tuned. This strategy opens up a new path to MOF synthesis especially structure control, providing new insights into how to utilize solid matters with specific morphologies and architectures as metal sources to realize certain fabrication. (3) Furthermore, one can also be interested in fabricating the HoMS & MOF hybrid, which means both the inorganic metal oxide (also can be sulphide, etc.) and MOF are existed in different shells of a single HoMS particle. In this case, this hybrid system may be beneficial for the shape-selective catalysis and cascade reaction. Thus, it can give full play to the exact temporal–spatial control, which will provide a new catalytic schematic approach for industrial synthesis. (4) Last but not the least, we would like to highlight another possibility of MOF-decorated HoMSs. The last few years have witnessed the booming of utilizing MOFs for the preparation of single atom catalysts (SAC) to achieve large loading amount and high dispersion of atomically active sites.78- 80 It is reasonable to believe that loading SAs on HoMS can create a cooperative activation from both the site-specific single atoms and multiple hollowness. In summary, besides performing as the star material for various applications, MOFs can also bring lots of foundations for the precise synthesis of HoMS, such as the variousVimewoArrptichleolOonlginye, the relative lower metal element gradienDt,OtI:h1e0.1o0r3d9e/rDe0dSCp0o1r2o8u4Js structure and the topological ordered metallic atom arrangement. In reverse, HoMSs can bring MOFs the enhanced mass transport and effective surface exposure, especially the temporal-spatial ordering property. The combination of MOFs and HoMSs is promising because it can not only enhance the properties of the materials but also endow them with new functions. Although there are still many difficulties challenging us, researchers across the world have flocked into this research hotspot. Many more breakthroughs and progresses in both the synthesis and applications can be expected in the near future. Hopefully, the insight gained from this perspective article will aid in the rational design and synthesis of other multi-shelled hollow structures and the further e